'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Finding a white coating on a dry-cured chorizo or salchichón can be confusing. On some dry sausages, surface mould is part of controlled maturation; on other products, unexpected growth after purchase may point to poor storage. The important limitation is that <strong>“it is white” is not enough to decide whether a mould is desirable or safe</strong>.</p>
<p>The answer depends on the type of sausage, how it was made, its packaging and moisture level, the producer's instructions and whether the coating was intentionally present from the start or appeared unexpectedly at home.</p>
<h2>Why do some cured sausages have mould?</h2>
<p>During fermentation and drying, certain salamis and cured sausages develop surface mould. Some producers intentionally apply selected cultures. They can occupy the surface, help suppress unwanted growth and contribute to maturation and aroma.</p>
<p>The US Food Safety and Inspection Service explains that <strong>dry white mould is often found on fermented and dried sausages</strong> and can be desirable in specific products. It also notes that some traditional salamis characteristically carry a thin white coating.</p>
<p>This does not mean every chorizo or salchichón should be mouldy. Many products are sold with a clean casing, peeled or vacuum packed with no visible bloom. A product that was clean when purchased and later develops new patches should be assessed differently.</p>
<h2>A uniform white bloom is not the same as unexpected patches</h2>
<p>A maturation bloom is generally <strong>thin, dry and relatively uniform</strong> and is part of the producer's intended appearance. Growth that appears during home storage—especially alongside excess moisture, condensation, an abnormal smell or texture changes—should not automatically be labelled “good mould”.</p>
<p>Colour cannot identify a fungal species. FSIS specifically notes that <strong>colour is not necessarily an indicator of “good” or “bad” mould</strong>. Internet rules such as “white is safe, green means discard” are therefore too simplistic.</p>
<h2>First question: what kind of product is it?</h2>
<p>The same rule cannot be applied to every meat product. A whole dry salchichón is very different from fresh sausage, cooked deli meat or moist sliced meat stored after opening.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Whole dry-cured sausage:</strong> certain surface moulds can be normal characteristics.</li>
<li><strong>Fresh sausage:</strong> mould is not part of its normal production and should not be treated like mould on dry salami.</li>
<li><strong>Cooked or high-moisture meat:</strong> mould can extend below the visible surface and safety guidance is stricter.</li>
<li><strong>Packaged slices:</strong> visible growth inside a package intended to stay clean is different from a traditional surface bloom on a whole sausage.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Can you eat the casing with the white coating?</h2>
<p>That depends on the casing and the product. Some sausages use <strong>edible natural casing</strong>, others use edible collagen, and some have an outer covering intended to be removed. A maturation bloom does not automatically make the casing itself edible.</p>
<p>Check the label or producer information. Our guide to <a href="/en/can-you-eat-chorizo-salchichon-casing-natural-collagen-other-casings/">whether chorizo and salchichón casing is edible</a> explains the main casing types.</p>
<h2>Can mould be cleaned from a dry-cured sausage?</h2>
<p>Food-safety authorities recognise that characteristic surface mould can be removed from certain dry, shelf-stable products. FSIS guidance allows surface mould to be scrubbed from specific hard salamis and dry-cured hams. But this applies to <strong>particular dry, stable products</strong>, not to every mouldy food.</p>
<p>If the bloom is the sausage's normal coating, removing the casing before slicing may also be appropriate, depending on the producer's directions. Unexpected, extensive or wet growth accompanied by other abnormalities is not necessarily solved by wiping the surface.</p>
<h2>Signs that make discarding the product the safer choice</h2>
<p>Discard the product or contact the producer when several of these signs appear:</p>
<ul>
<li>mould that was not originally present and is spreading quickly;</li>
<li>colour or texture unlike the product's characteristic bloom;</li>
<li>a wet, slimy or tacky surface on a sausage that should be dry;</li>
<li>a distinctly putrid, rancid or abnormal odour;</li>
<li>a vacuum pack that has lost its seal, swollen or leaked;</li>
<li>heavy condensation combined with storage outside label instructions;</li>
<li>mould on fresh, cooked or high-moisture meat products.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not put your nose close to a mould colony to “test” its smell. Spores can cause respiratory or allergic problems in sensitive people. A doubtful product should be judged by appearance, packaging and storage history, not by deliberately inhaling the mould.</p>
<h2>Why can mould appear after opening chorizo?</h2>
<p>After opening, the product encounters oxygen, ambient humidity, utensils and surfaces. Storage that is too humid for that particular sausage may encourage new growth. An environment that is too dry, on the other hand, can harden the outside rapidly.</p>
<p>Storage depends on whether the product is whole, partly eaten or sliced. See our guide to <a href="/en/how-long-do-chorizo-salchichon-cured-loin-last-after-opening-storage/">how long chorizo, salchichón and cured loin last after opening</a> for the practical storage framework.</p>
<h2>White mould versus white specks inside the sausage</h2>
<p>Another common mistake is calling every white spot mould. Inside salchichón, visible white pieces may simply be <strong>fat</strong>, crystals or normal components of the mixture. Surface mould grows on the casing or an exposed surface.</p>
<p>Location therefore matters. An external film is not interpreted in the same way as firm white flecks integrated into the cut surface. Moist cavities, abnormal colours or obviously altered internal texture deserve more caution.</p>
<h2>Should cured sausage be wrapped tightly in plastic?</h2>
<p>Tight plastic around a product that is still releasing moisture can encourage condensation. Leaving it completely exposed can dry it excessively and expose it to odours or contamination. There is no universal rule because diameter, water activity, casing and degree of curing differ.</p>
<p>Follow the producer's storage instructions first. If the product is sold refrigerated or the label says refrigerate after opening, that instruction takes priority. A dry shelf-stable sausage may have different guidance.</p>
<h2>People at higher risk</h2>
<p>Ready-to-eat raw cured meat products require additional caution for people at increased risk of foodborne infection. Spanish food-safety guidance advises certain vulnerable groups to avoid raw cured ready-to-eat meat unless it receives the specified heat treatment. In those circumstances, deciding whether a surface bloom is traditional does not replace the specific advice for the risk group.</p>
<h2>The practical rule: do not judge by white colour alone</h2>
<p>A <strong>thin, expected white bloom</strong> can be part of the maturation of certain dry sausages. But colour alone cannot identify a mould or guarantee its safety. Check the product type, its original appearance, storage history and producer instructions. Unexpected growth accompanied by moisture, offensive odour, slime or package failure is a reason not to eat it.</p>
